  "evidence": "TEXT_E: Five pledges focused on class sizes (education), NHS waiting lists, youth crime processing times, jobs for under-25s, and no rise in basic/top income tax; primary-care-led NHS reform with real-terms funding increases; welfare-to-work programme; minimum wage via Low Pay Commission. TEXT_F: Emphasis on tight public spending, tax rate ambitions, EU opt-outs, privatisation (rail, London Underground, Royal Mail options), selection/grammar schools expansion, and law-and-order measures; NHS real-terms increases but within internal market; welfare-to-work expansion.",
  "reasoning": "In 1997, voters’ top concerns were the NHS, education standards/class sizes, jobs/unemployment (especially youth), crime, and reassurance on income tax, which TEXT_E addresses directly with clear, measurable pledges and funding plans. TEXT_F prioritises structural market reforms, privatisation, and EU positions that were less central to mass voter salience, and offers fewer concrete service-delivery commitments on the NHS and schools that match those priorities.",
  "confidence": "high",
  "winner": "TEXT_E"
}
